"""Original passages. Short versions are complete paragraphs; long versions retain narrative context."""
STORIES = [
('The garden gate', '''Mina noticed that the community garden gate was open. A small rabbit was nibbling the lettuce inside. She could not see the gardener. Mina closed the gate gently after guiding the rabbit outside with a trail of leaves. Then she waited beside the fence. When the gardener arrived, she explained what had happened. He thanked her for protecting the plants without frightening the rabbit.''', '''The next Saturday, Mina returned to help plant beans. Before she started, she checked the latch. It slid into place but slipped out again when she pushed the gate. Now she understood why the rabbit had entered. The gardener, Tomas, brought a small toolbox. He showed Mina the loose screw holding the latch to the post. She held the metal plate steady while he tightened it. They tested the gate together three times. Each time it stayed shut. Mina wrote a short note asking visitors to check the latch as they left. She drew a rabbit beside the words, hoping the picture would remind people why the gate mattered.''', '''A week later, Mina found a new problem. A visitor using a wheelchair had difficulty reaching the high latch. The visitor, Leena, could push the gate easily once it was open, but she needed someone else to let her through. Mina felt pleased with the repair until Leena explained this. A gate that kept rabbits out also needed to welcome every gardener.
Mina asked Tomas whether they could simply lower the latch. He explained that a low latch might be easy for younger children to open without an adult. They needed to consider more than one person's needs. Leena suggested a longer handle connected to the existing mechanism. Tomas sketched the idea on the back of an old seed packet. Mina measured the gate while Leena tried different handle positions using a strip of cardboard.
They made a temporary handle and invited several visitors to test it. One handle caught on a sleeve. Another was difficult to grip with wet hands. The third design had a broad, smooth loop. Visitors could reach it from different heights, and it returned securely after use. They chose that version, then tested the latch again.
Mina changed her note. Instead of only warning people about rabbits, it welcomed visitors and explained how the new handle worked. Leena added a simple drawing. Tomas placed the note where people could read it before reaching the gate.
At the next planting day, Mina saw Leena enter without waiting for help. Behind her, a family carefully checked that the gate had closed. Across the path, the rabbit ate clover near the hedge. Mina realised that solving a problem was sometimes the beginning of a better question. She had first asked how to keep a rabbit outside. Listening to Leena had helped her ask how to keep the garden open to everyone.'''),
('The lantern walk', '''Arun was making a lantern for the neighbourhood walk. His first paper cover tore when he pulled it over the frame. He wanted to throw it away. His sister Jo suggested using the torn paper to practise a smaller fold. Arun tried again more slowly. The new cover fitted. He saved the scraps in a box for someone else to use.''', '''On the evening of the walk, a gust of wind lifted several decorations from the meeting table. Arun held his lantern close while Jo gathered the loose paper. A younger child named Bea stared at her empty frame. Her cover had blown into a puddle. Arun remembered the box of scraps at home. He could not fetch it before the walk began, but Jo had kept some spare pieces in her bag. Together they made a patchwork cover. Bea chose a blue triangle for the window and a yellow circle for the moon. It looked different from the other lanterns, which pleased her.''', '''The organisers announced that the group would follow the riverside path. Arun had practised carrying his lantern along the short route behind his house. The riverside walk was longer, and the wind was stronger there. He asked whether Bea would manage with her repaired cover. Bea said she wanted to try, but she would like to stay near someone she knew.
As they walked, Arun watched the blue and yellow patches glow around the small battery light. He had wanted his own lantern to look perfect. Now he noticed how many different designs surrounded him. One had painted leaves. Another had a crooked star. An older neighbour carried a plain jar that caught reflections from every passing light.
At the narrow bridge, the group slowed down. Someone behind Arun called for everyone to hurry. Jo asked them to wait while a family crossed safely. Bea held her lantern in both hands and watched the water below. Arun stood beside her rather than pulling her forward. When she was ready, they walked across at her pace.
The path ended at a small park. The organisers invited people to place their lanterns in a wide circle, leaving room for everyone to see. Bea put hers beside Arun's. A neighbour asked where she had bought the unusual cover. Bea laughed and explained how it had been made. Arun almost mentioned that his own first cover had torn, but Bea had already begun telling the story of the blue window and yellow moon.
On the way home, Jo asked which part of the evening he would remember. Arun thought about the circle of lights and the quiet pause on the bridge. He said that a lantern did not have to be perfect to help someone find their way. The next morning, he put the unused scraps beside the craft table with a note inviting people to make something new. He left his carefully folded lantern there as well, ready for another walk.'''),
('A place for the birds', '''Ella put a bowl of water on the balcony during a hot week. No birds visited on the first day. Her cousin Ben thought they should move it beside the noisy doorway. Ella suggested waiting and watching instead. The next morning, a small bird landed near the quiet corner. Ella made a note of the time before refilling the bowl.''', '''For four mornings, Ella watched from inside the window. She recorded the time, the weather and whether a bird visited. On the fourth morning, two birds arrived together. Ben wanted to announce that the bowl was a success. Ella pointed out that four days were only a small sample. They had not watched every hour, and they could not know whether the same birds returned. They decided to keep observing without disturbing the visitors. Ella asked an adult to help clean the bowl each evening and replace the water. Ben drew a simple chart with room for another week of observations.''', '''Their neighbour Samira noticed the chart when she came to visit. She explained that birds also needed shelter from predators and a clear view of their surroundings. A bowl could be clean and full yet still be in an unsuitable place. Ella showed her the quiet corner. A tall pot partly blocked the view from one side. With an adult's help, they moved the pot away without shifting the water bowl.
The following week was cooler. Fewer birds appeared in the mornings. Ben wondered whether moving the pot had caused the change. Ella looked at the weather notes. Perhaps the birds needed less water in cooler conditions, or perhaps they were visiting at another time. Their chart could suggest questions, but it could not answer all of them. They added a short afternoon observation on the weekend.
When their class began a project about helping local wildlife, Ben wanted to use a large heading that said their balcony had saved dozens of birds. Ella disagreed. They had counted visits, not individual birds, and had no evidence that any bird had been in danger. Samira helped them choose a more accurate heading: Watching Birds at a Balcony Water Bowl. Under it, they described what they had done and what they still did not know.
At school, some classmates found the cautious title less exciting than Ben's first idea. Then they began asking questions. How often was the bowl cleaned? Was there shade? Could the same bird visit several times? Ben answered using their notes. Ella explained that their small project was useful because other people could repeat the observations and compare them.
That afternoon, Ben added one final sentence to the display. It said that careful records helped them notice birds without pretending to know everything about them. Ella left space below it for future questions. Back at home, a bird rested on the balcony rail. Neither cousin rushed towards the window. They watched quietly, then wrote down what they had actually seen.'''),
]
def passage(index,minimum,maximum):
 title,short,middle,long=STORIES[index%3]
 parts=[short]
 if len(short.split())<minimum:parts.append(middle)
 if len(' '.join(parts).split())<minimum:
  for paragraph in long.split('\n'):
   if paragraph.strip():parts.append(paragraph.strip())
   if len(' '.join(parts).split())>=minimum:break
 result='\n\n'.join(parts)
 assert minimum<=len(result.split())<=maximum,(title,len(result.split()),minimum,maximum)
 return title,result
